 \section{Discussion}
-\subsection{Interpretation and limits of reference-budget effects}
-The ca-GrQc result changes the interpretation of an apparent budget effect. At default PageRank tolerance, increasing the shared selection budget changes the restricted-minus-full mean difference from positive to negative. Both seed-selection procedures vary with $W$, so this contrast cannot be attributed solely to an improving full reference. Tightening tolerance exchanges two pool members and removes that pattern, although increasing $W$ still changes selected sets. Thus, the default reversal is not evidence for a budget effect that is independent of ranking precision. The candidate cutoff makes numerical ranking differences relevant to seed eligibility. Losses persist on CollegeMsg and wiki-Vote, so this explanation does not account for every unfavorable PageRank comparison.
-
-StaticGreedy already makes reuse of sampled realizations central to a consistent finite objective \cite{ref8}. Our fixed-edge control follows the same coverage principle, whereas the primary step-indexed coupling can produce a nonmonotone finite objective. This distinction concerns the sampled construction and does not contradict the expected-spread result of Kempe et al. \cite{ref1}. The additional issue identified here occurs before selection: numerical tolerance can change which vertices enter a truncated pool. Shared worlds control the comparison between eligible sets, but do not remove this dependence on pool construction.
-
-\subsection{From seed agreement to a quality--cost judgment}
-Benchmarking work examines influence-maximization quality together with computational cost \cite{ref16}. Our paired comparisons add a distinction between preserving a reference's seeds and preserving its independently evaluated utility. A changed trajectory may end at the same set, which gives the same evaluation vector under shared simulation worlds. When sets differ, paired evaluation measures the sign, magnitude and conditional uncertainty of their utility difference. The external ca-GrQc mean change of 0.12720 nodes and the historical step-coupled mean change of 0.01111 nodes illustrate distinct budget transitions. Neither value defines an application-specific equivalence margin.
-
-This distinction changes how a reported speed saving should be read. The degree-pool results identify computation saved while preserving the tested reference. The PageRank results require the corresponding utility loss to be considered alongside that saving. Candidate fraction, ranking tolerance and the shared selection budget specify the quality comparison; included timing stages specify the cost comparison. Reporting these settings connects the measured computational savings to their associated spread differences.
-
-\subsection{Network direction and transfer of ranking results}
-The wiki-Vote observations are consistent with a mismatch between incoming importance and outgoing propagation. The PageRank pool emphasizes vertices with many incoming links, while diffusion in this study follows outgoing arcs. Direction was not experimentally reversed, so the diagnostic does not isolate a causal mechanism. Network structure, candidate coverage and the propagation setting remain relevant to the observed loss. The broader distinction between a simulated objective and an empirical influence model also matters \cite{ref15}. Agreement on contact graphs cannot by itself establish that a ranking will preserve utility on differently directed networks. The increase in PageRank coverage improves the measured comparison, but does not eliminate the wiki-Vote loss.
-
-\subsection{Scope, limitations and next tests}
-The external evidence covers three selected networks under $k=5$, $p=.01$, $h=3$ and selection budgets up to 1280. Historical contact graphs also probe $p$, $h$, coupling and $k$, but their local parameter changes are not a full factorial design and do not establish sensitivity on the larger directed graphs. At historical $k=20$, the 4k pool contains all 75 Kenya vertices and 80/91 invs2013 vertices; Kenya agreement is a full-pool identity check. The historical fixed-edge controls also do not validate the external tighter-tolerance PageRank results under fixed-edge sampling.
-
-Paths repeat computations on a fixed graph, and each synthetic control uses one graph instance. Conditional Monte Carlo uncertainty concerns fixed selected sets, while path variation describes computational repetitions. The selected networks provide observed topologies for the specified diffusion model.
-
-Reference agreement measures fidelity to the sampled greedy solution. The cost comparison uses this implementation's exhaustive full-candidate reference. Measured selection timings and assembled warm-process costs describe distinct usage scenarios; their components are specified in Methods.
-
-\paragraph{Next tests.} Two targeted studies follow from these limits. A joint tolerance--coupling control on the external networks would test whether the precision-sensitive interpretation persists with fixed-edge worlds. A direction intervention, with the remaining settings held constant, would help distinguish the proposed directional explanation from other structural differences. Broader external probability and horizon settings are also needed before extending the contact-graph sensitivity results.
+\subsection{A budget comparison can depend on candidate-pool precision}
+The ca-GrQc reversal disappears after two candidates are exchanged by a tighter PageRank tolerance. This changes the interpretation of the default budget scan. At fixed $W$, the full reference and evaluation worlds are unchanged, so the tolerance comparison isolates the consequence of the revised restricted pool. Across $W$, both methods select anew. The remaining budget-dependent seed changes therefore concern selection within each pool, whereas the lost mean sign reversal depends on which pool is used. The positive strict-tolerance mean at $W=1280$ also combines positive and negative path differences; it does not establish a uniform advantage.
+
+StaticGreedy uses shared fixed realizations to maintain a consistent sampled objective \cite{ref8}. The present comparison adds an earlier computational step: numerical ranking determines eligibility before that objective is searched. Fixing the sampled worlds cannot hold eligibility constant when the truncated ranking changes. The two controls answer different questions and both matter for interpreting this example. Our fixed-edge control follows the coverage construction; the step-indexed objective has the finite-sample limitation described in Methods. These sampled objectives must be distinguished from the expected-spread guarantee of Kempe et al. \cite{ref1}.
+
+\subsection{Seed differences need an independent utility scale}
+On ca-GrQc, all five reference sets change between $W=320$ and 1280, yet their mean spread change is 0.12720 nodes. On wiki-Vote, some selection orders change while the final sets and evaluated spread remain identical. These observations separate two sources of apparent instability: changing the order of the same seeds and changing the seeds themselves. Even in the latter case, the signed utility difference requires measurement. The historical mean of 0.01111 nodes accompanies 16 changed sets across 40 pairs and includes both positive and negative changes.
+
+Quality--cost benchmarking compares achieved influence with computation \cite{ref16}. Here, reporting the actual spread alongside agreement distinguishes preservation of the sampled solution from similarity in utility. The wiki-Vote example makes the distinction concrete: the degree pool reproduces the reference seeds, whereas the PageRank pools return different seeds and lower spread. Across all five paths, enlarging the default PageRank pool raises mean total spread from 20.04268 to 47.20590, against 60.73602 for full selection. An agreement count alone would miss the size of that recovery.
+
+\subsection{Coverage and direction explain different parts of the comparison}
+The default wiki-Vote PageRank-4k pool excludes every full-reference seed at $W=1280$; the 5\% pool contains only one. Reproducing the full set is therefore impossible within either pool. Increasing coverage still reduces incremental loss from 73.01\% to 24.28\%, showing that membership overlap and recovered utility describe different outcomes. The degree pool contains and selects the reference seeds with only 20 candidates. Pool size alone does not explain the contrast between the two rankings.
+
+The structural diagnostic is consistent with the direction of the ranking and propagation: PageRank candidates have higher mean in-degree, while degree candidates have higher mean out-degree. Diffusion follows outgoing arcs in this experiment. Because direction was not intervened on, this remains an explanation consistent with the observations rather than an isolated causal effect. The simulated objective also differs from estimating influence from observed adoption, as in empirical influence modelling \cite{ref15}.
+
+Selection-time savings have different meanings for these outputs. Degree-4k saves time while reproducing the tested reference; the PageRank savings accompany the reported spread losses. Setup and independent evaluation reduce the savings when included in assembled costs. The relevant comparison therefore depends on whether a user needs seed selection with prepared worlds or the full evaluated workflow.
+
+\subsection{Scope and unresolved comparisons}
+The external sample comprises three graphs with $k=5$, $p=.01$, $h=3$ and $W\le1280$. Historical contact graphs extend parameter and coupling coverage, but do not establish the same sensitivity on larger directed networks. At historical $k=20$, Kenya's 4k pool is the complete 75-node graph and invs2013 retains 80/91 vertices. These high-coverage cases provide limited evidence for aggressive candidate reduction.
+
+The precision control is post-hoc and reuses the original five paths. Paths describe computational variation on each graph; conditional Monte Carlo errors describe fixed seed sets. Reference agreement is with a sampled greedy solution, and timings compare this implementation's exhaustive selection. The saved experiments leave two specific explanations unresolved: whether the external precision result persists under fixed-edge coupling, and whether changing propagation direction changes the directed-network ranking contrast. Wider external probability and horizon settings would also be required to assess transfer of the historical parameter controls.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-Candidate-restriction quality depends on finite-world seed selection in both methods and on the ranking that defines the restricted pool. Increasing $W$ changes both selection procedures; independently evaluated relative differences therefore describe their joint response to the selection budget. On ca-GrQc, tightening PageRank tolerance removes the apparent budget-induced sign reversal, while budget-dependent seed changes remain. Separate reference-transition comparisons distinguish changes in seed membership from the direction and magnitude of estimated spread changes. Across the external sample, Degree-4k preserves the tested $W=1280$ trajectories at lower measured selection cost, whereas PageRank outcomes depend on precision, coverage and network context. Evaluation reports should distinguish the shared selection budget, the independent evaluation budget and candidate-pool construction, and report signed spread differences alongside scoped costs. These findings apply to the networks, propagation settings and implementations examined here.
+The paired comparisons identify a ranking-precision dependence in a budget-based quality judgment and quantify the spread consequences of seed changes. On ca-GrQc, the default mean reversal disappears after the candidate pool changes under tighter tolerance; budget-dependent seed changes remain. Independent evaluation distinguishes these changes from their signed utility effects. On the external networks, degree pools preserve the highest-budget reference at lower selection cost, while PageRank illustrates losses and partial recovery through greater coverage. For the tested simulation settings, reporting candidate membership, both methods' selection budget, independent spread and scoped costs makes the quality of candidate compression assessable.
 
